00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.730
SETH VARGO: In this episode
of The Secret Manager video

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:06.630
series, we'll talk about the
principle of least privilege

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:10.860
and how you can limit who
can access your secrets.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:13.530
Do you want to limit
access to your secrets?

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:15.060
Stay tuned to find out how.

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:18.546
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:27.540
The principle of least privilege
says that an entity must only

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:30.840
be able to access the
information or resources

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:34.080
necessary to perform
its function.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:36.330
In the context of
Secret Manager,

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:40.560
this means humans and machines
should only have access

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:44.140
to the secrets that are required
to perform their function.

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:48.150
For example, a security
engineer may have full control

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:52.590
to create and manage secrets,
but an application developer

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:57.030
may only have permission
to access a single secret.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:59.070
But how do we accomplish this?

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:02.310
To tell us more about how to
adopt the principle of least

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:04.435
privilege with Secret
Manager, I think

00:01:04.435 --> 00:01:05.685
we need to bring in an expert.

00:01:05.685 --> 00:01:08.580
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:10.110
Hey, Nick.

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:11.423
Thanks for joining us today.

00:01:11.423 --> 00:01:12.590
NICK MUNARRIZ: Thanks, Seth.

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:15.270
Thank you so much for having me.

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:18.560
SETH VARGO: So Nick is a
software engineer at Google

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:20.900
who works on Secret Manager.

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:23.810
Nick, earlier I was
telling our viewers

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:27.140
about the principle
of least privilege.

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:29.180
Could you tell us
a little bit more

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:32.657
about how Secret Manager helps
users adopt this principle?

00:01:32.657 --> 00:01:33.740
NICK MUNARRIZ: Sure, Seth.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:35.150
So on Google Cloud,
most controls

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:38.600
are implemented using Cloud
Identity and Access Management,

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.670
or IAM.

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:43.340
IAM provides very
granular permissions

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:45.320
that are combined into roles.

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:50.530
Secret Manager currently offers
five granular Cloud IAM roles.

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:53.800
First is the Secret
Manager Admin IAM role,

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:56.590
which grants full access to
administer Secret Manager

00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:00.990
resources, including creating
and deleting secrets.

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:04.200
Then the Secret
Manager Viewer IAM role

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.050
grants permission to
view the secret metadata

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:10.500
on Secret Manager resources, but
it does not include the ability

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.480
to access secret payloads.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.300
This is useful for
auditing secrets

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:19.510
without actually seeing
the payloads inside.

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:23.170
Then the Secret Manager
Secret Accessor IAM role

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:25.820
grants the ability to
access secret payloads,

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:29.030
but does not grant any
administrative permissions.

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:31.540
This is useful for humans
and applications that

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:33.790
need to access a secret,
but don't need permissions

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:36.460
to manage those secrets.

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:41.020
The Secret Manager
Version Manager IAM role

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:44.570
grants permission to manage
versions of existing secrets,

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:46.270
but it does not
include the ability

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:49.450
to access secret payloads
or view or update

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:53.010
the metadata on any
secrets themselves.

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:57.665
Finally, the Secret Manager
Version Adder IAM role grants

00:02:57.665 --> 00:03:00.940
permissions to add new
versions to existing secrets,

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:02.420
but it does not
include the ability

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:06.680
to access secret payloads or
manage any versions or secrets.

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:08.690
This is useful for
applications that might need

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:11.500
to perform simple rotations.

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:14.470
SETH VARGO: OK, so it sounds
like Secret Manager has

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:19.000
this really nice separation
of concerns between managing

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:22.630
secrets, auditing
secrets, rotating secrets,

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:24.550
and accessing secrets.

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:27.640
So this will definitely
help me adopt that principle

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:28.970
of least privilege.

00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:32.680
But wouldn't granting someone
Secret Manager Secret Accessor

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:36.070
permissions give
them access to all

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:37.853
of the secrets in my project?

00:03:37.853 --> 00:03:39.520
NICK MUNARRIZ: So
that really depends on

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:42.130
where you grant
that IAM permission.

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:45.250
Secret Manager's IAM roles
can be applied globally

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:48.430
on a whole project or
individually on just

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:49.990
a single secret.

00:03:49.990 --> 00:03:52.300
If you grant an
entity, like a user,

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:55.120
the Secret Manager
Secret Accessor role

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:57.940
on the project, then
that entity can access

00:03:57.940 --> 00:04:00.200
all the secrets in the project.

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:02.950
But if you grant the Secret
Manager Secret Accessor

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:05.200
to just an entity
on a single secret,

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:08.900
then the entity will only be
able to access that one secret.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:11.390
SETH VARGO: OK, so it
sounds like if I really

00:04:11.390 --> 00:04:13.790
want to follow that
principle of least privilege,

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:17.633
I should apply IAM roles
at the most granular level.

00:04:17.633 --> 00:04:18.800
NICK MUNARRIZ: That's right.

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:21.560
Since Cloud IAM's default
policy is to deny,

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:23.750
applying the roles at
the most granular level

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:26.083
is what really helps you
achieve that principle of least

00:04:26.083 --> 00:04:27.890
privilege.

00:04:27.890 --> 00:04:29.000
SETH VARGO: OK, awesome.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.880
But Google Cloud has
these built-in roles,

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:35.220
like Project Editor
and Project Owner.

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:38.060
How do those built-in roles
map to these Secret Manager

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:39.315
permissions?

00:04:39.315 --> 00:04:41.190
NICK MUNARRIZ: That's
a really good question.

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:43.130
So project owners are
kind of super users,

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:46.460
so they have full permissions
to access and manage secrets.

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:47.955
Even if we remove
this permission,

00:04:47.955 --> 00:04:49.580
they could just grant
it to themselves,

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:54.395
because they're also full
IAM policy administrators.

00:04:54.395 --> 00:04:55.770
SETH VARGO: OK,
that makes sense.

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:58.200
We could take it away,
but they could just

00:04:58.200 --> 00:04:59.197
add it back themselves.

00:04:59.197 --> 00:05:00.030
NICK MUNARRIZ: Yeah.

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:03.000
However, perhaps unlike
other GCP services,

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:05.100
the ability to access
secrets and the ability

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:07.080
to change IAM
permissions on secrets

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:10.200
is not actually granted to
the Project Editor role,

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:13.990
and that includes the default
Compute Engine Service account.

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:16.290
This means the only
Project Owners and entities

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:19.013
who are explicitly granted
permissions can access secrets

00:05:19.013 --> 00:05:19.680
in your project.

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:23.140
SETH VARGO: Awesome.

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:24.610
Thank you so much, Nick.

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:27.370
This really makes it
clearer how Secret Manager

00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:31.180
can help to adopt the
principle of least privilege.

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:32.260
OK.

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:34.420
Let's take a look
at how to practice

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:38.260
the principle of least privilege
with Google Secret Manager.

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:41.710
I created a secret that
contains my Twilio API key,

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:44.650
and I need to grant my
Cloud Function permissions

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:46.990
to access that secret.

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:50.830
Now, I could go to
the Cloud IAM settings

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:53.410
and grant my Cloud
Function Service account

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:56.740
the Secret Manager
Accessor permissions.

00:05:56.740 --> 00:06:00.880
But as we learned from Nick,
that isn't a best practice,

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:04.840
because that would allow my
Cloud Function to access all

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:07.420
of the secrets in the project.

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:11.170
Instead, we want to follow the
principle of least privilege

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:13.690
and grant access
only to the secrets

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:15.730
that my Cloud Function needs.

00:06:15.730 --> 00:06:20.170
To do this, I'll head over to
the Secret Manager Console,

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:27.070
select my secret, expand the
Info panel, click Add Member,

00:06:27.070 --> 00:06:30.070
and grant my Cloud Function
Service account permissions

00:06:30.070 --> 00:06:31.990
to access the secret.

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:36.460
Here, I apply the IAM
policy at the secret level,

00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:38.920
meaning my Cloud Function
won't have access

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:41.700
to the other secrets
in my project.

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:44.730
I could also grant this
permission using the gcloud

00:06:44.730 --> 00:06:49.620
command line tool, or from the
API using any of the Secret

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:57.910
Manager SDKs for .NET, Go, Java,
Node, PHP, Python, and Ruby.

00:06:57.910 --> 00:07:01.780
Google Secret Manager makes
it easy to adopt and practice

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:03.370
the principle of
least privilege.

